        <history>
          <p>Composed in the aftermath of the First World War, the Fifth Symphony is built from two large movements rather than the traditional four and famously calls for a side-drum player instructed to improvise ‘as if to disturb the music at all costs’ — a starkly modern gesture that has made the symphony a landmark of the twentieth-century orchestral repertoire.</p>
